The fundamental idea is proximity. Network latency grows with distance, so serving a file from an edge server a few milliseconds away instead of an origin hundreds of milliseconds away makes pages and apps feel instant. The CDN has many edge locations worldwide, and a request is routed to the nearest healthy one, usually via DNS or anycast routing.

Caching works on a time-to-live. When an edge does not have a requested file, it fetches it from the origin once, stores it, and serves all subsequent requests from its cache until the time-to-live expires. This is why the origin load drops dramatically; it serves each file once per edge per expiry period rather than once per user.

```java
// Edge cache logic: serve from edge, fetch from origin on miss
public byte[] serve(String url) {
    CacheEntry entry = edgeCache.get(url);
    if (entry != null && !entry.isExpired()) return entry.body();  // edge HIT
    byte[] body = origin.fetch(url);                                // MISS → origin
    edgeCache.put(url, new CacheEntry(body, ttlSeconds));
    return body;
}
```

The cache key decides what counts as the same object. By default it is the URL, but it can include query strings or headers when content varies, so you must be careful that a cache key does not accidentally serve one user's personalized content to another. Invalidation removes stale content before its time-to-live naturally expires. Two approaches are explicit invalidation requests, which purge a path from all edges, and versioned URLs, where a changed file gets a new name like logo-v2.png so clients simply request a new, uncached object; versioned URLs are generally preferred because they avoid the delay and cost of purging.

What belongs on a CDN is cacheable content: images, stylesheets, scripts, fonts, and video segments, which are identical for every user. Dynamic, personalized content like a specific account balance should not be cached at the edge, or must use a very short time-to-live and a cache key that isolates each user. A senior nuance is securing premium content with signed, time-limited URLs so only authorized users fetch it, and using the CDN for availability and denial-of-service protection, since the distributed edge absorbs volumetric attacks far better than a single origin.

